Falsifies `kernel::arch::aarch64::iommu::proofs::no_stream_can_reach_another_streams_tables`.

The defect is the change this file most invites a future reader to make. `STRTAB_LOG2` is
6 and its comment says so almost apologetically ("64 covers bus 0's 32 devices twice
over"); a second PCIe bus, or a board whose `iommu-map` is not the identity, and raising
it to 7 is the obvious edit. It is also wrong, and nothing else in the tree says so: 128
entries at a 64-byte stride is 8192 bytes, and both the stream table and the
context-descriptor frame are **one** page frame from `zeroed_page_frame`. Streams 64
through 127 have their entries written off the end of a kernel-owned frame into whatever
the allocator handed out next.

The patch is that edit, whole and self-consistent: `init` programs the larger
`STRTAB_BASE_CFG` too, so the SMMU agrees with the kernel about the table's size and
nothing complains.

**Every test in this tree stays green under it**, for the plain reason that the requester
id of the one PCIe disk on `virt` is far below 64, so no test ever addresses an entry past
the frame. The bound is the only thing standing between a device-tree-supplied `StreamID`
and a raw write, and this proves the bound and the stride have to be checked against each
other rather than separately.

Expected to fail: `assert!(oa + STE_BYTES <= page_frames::FRAME_SIZE, "every entry the
bound admits lies inside the single frame that holds the table")`. Confirmed red on
2026-09-04.

The sibling harness `the_smmu_is_handed_exactly_the_tables_the_kernel_built` stays green
under this patch: it takes `ctxptr` symbolic rather than deriving it from a `StreamID`, so
it says nothing about how many of them there may be.

diff --git a/kernel/src/arch/aarch64/iommu.rs b/kernel/src/arch/aarch64/iommu.rs
index 2769577a..c0d88399 100644
--- a/kernel/src/arch/aarch64/iommu.rs
+++ b/kernel/src/arch/aarch64/iommu.rs
@@ -72,7 +72,7 @@ const EVT_BYTES: u64 = 32;
 
 // The linear stream table: 64 STEs of 64 bytes, one frame. StreamID on this board is the PCIe
 // requester id (the DTB's iommu-map is identity), so 64 covers bus 0's 32 devices twice over.
-const STRTAB_LOG2: u32 = 6;
+const STRTAB_LOG2: u32 = 7;
 const STE_BYTES: u64 = 64;
 
 // Commands (opcode in byte 0 of word 0).
